Classic Python trap, live in our order service. Put this in /workspace/orders.py:

def add_item(sku, cart=[]):
    cart.append(sku)
    return cart

def new_order(customer):
    return {"customer": customer, "items": add_item("WELCOME-GIFT")}

Support says every customer's order contains every other customer's welcome gifts. Write tests/test_orders.py first: creating two orders for different customers must yield independent item lists each containing exactly one WELCOME-GIFT. Run pytest, confirm it fails, then explain the mutable-default-argument bug in a short comment, fix it with the None-sentinel idiom, and make the suite pass. Also add a regression test that calls add_item three times with no cart argument and asserts each returned list has length 1.

